china's biggest city shanghai has been  at a standstill for more than six weeks  but the strict pandemic lockdown may be  finally coming to an end for its 25  million people  starting from monday the commercial  center is gradually easing covert 19  restrictions aimed at resuming normal  life from the beginning of june the  mayor's office says infections which  began surging at the end of march are  now under control  yesterday the number of newly discovered  coveted 19 patients dropped below 1000  for the first time and there have been  no positive case reports at the  community level for two consecutive days  the epidemic situation in shanghai has  been effectively controlled  restrictions will be lifted in three  phases starting with the reopening of  some supermarkets pharmacies and  hairdressers  some restaurants will be allowed to  offer takeout but most restrictions on  people's movement will remain until next  week  china is fighting its worst outbreak  since the beginning of the pandemic  coronavirus restrictions have been  imposed in dozens of regions nationwide  including the capital  meanwhile in beijing authorities are  continuing to ask residents to mass test  every second day i'm just lining up now  for my 10th covert test in recent weeks  people living in most beijing districts  have been asked to work from home  china's leaders refuse to co-exist with  the virus and the economy is paying the  price  in april industrial output retail  spending and urban employment all fell  to their lowest levels in two years  home sales are down more than 30 since  last year  china's central bank has cut interest  rates for first-time buyers to help ease  the property slump  shanghai's leaders say it will take time  for everyone to completely resume normal  life  they're desperate to avoid a rebound in  infections as restrictions ease and  millions of residents in some areas  remain confined to their homes  katrina u al jazeera beijing
 

